function, the maximum value reached by the function is $f(x,y)=2.$ Therefore, the global maximum is $2.$ The function reaches this value when $(x,y)=(1,2),$ so the global maximum point is $(1,2,2).$\n\n![](images/69/690d407982bfa9cde8fdce5b187006af1c24f9080a0cfea77e7551ca1d5cd940.png)" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186121463 :answer-field/correct "answer-acec2003-faf8-5414-9547-98be22326726" :answer-field/choices ["answer-d277550a-a25f-5521-be5b-be206e9c7195" "answer-40d51ffd-3c87-5270-834d-5cb8714970fb" "answer-87e76412-257f-5583-a7e9-47e4ed2af022" "answer-acec2003-faf8-5414-9547-98be22326726" "answer-fd7d1370-1b00-5b14-93b1-c569a931408e"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-d277550a-a25f-5521-be5b-be206e9c7195" :answer/id #uuid "d277550a-a25f-5521-be5b-be206e9c7195" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$(-1,-2,1)$ only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-40d51ffd-3c87-5270-834d-5cb8714970fb" :answer/id #uuid "40d51ffd-3c87-5270-834d-5cb8714970fb" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$(1,2,2)$ and $(-1,-2,1)$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-87e76412-257f-5583-a7e9-47e4ed2af022" :answer/id #uuid "87e76412-257f-5583-a7e9-47e4ed2af022" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$(1,2,2)$ and $(0,-1,0)$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-acec2003-faf8-5414-9547-98be22326726" :answer/id #uuid "acec2003-faf8-5414-9547-98be22326726" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$(1,2,2)$ only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-fd7d1370-1b00-5b14-93b1-c569a931408e" :answer/id #uuid "fd7d1370-1b00-5b14-93b1-c569a931408e" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$(2,3,0)$ only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-e313337f-f7bb-58d5-96bd-6538b4821d8f" :question/id #uuid "e313337f-f7bb-58d5-96bd-6538b4821d8f" :question/math-academy-id "q-163835" :question/problem "![](images/58/58898653e07df36c7de1eac79aa9ce012e871c5323a344381645333eb1695adb.png)\n\nThe graph of the function $z=f(x,y)$ is shown above.\nWhat is the global maximum of the function?" :question/worked-solution "First, we recall the following definitions:\n\n- The global maximum of a function is the largest value it attains on its domain. There can only be (at most) one global maximum.\n\n- A global maximum point is a point $(x,y,z)$ where a function reaches a global maximum. There can be zero, one, or many global maximum points.\n\nFor our function, the maximum value reached by the function is $f(x,y)=1.5.$ Therefore, the global maximum is $1.5.$ The function reaches this value at both $(x,y)=(0,-2)$ and $(x,y)=(-2,1).$\n\n![](images/4b/4b8b56801adbffd5dd65b6e110593fdc0141b70315306c67ec7ed5842ae7f7d9.png)" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-04f38069-1590-557e-918a-2e6f1810787d"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-04f38069-1590-557e-918a-2e6f1810787d" :answer-field/id #uuid "04f38069-1590-557e-918a-2e6f1810787d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-b427e1ff-6b3d-5b12-b7a7-f23687f41949" :answer-field/choices ["answer-94d307ae-d9c4-5198-958c-581b3e680cb8" "answer-4b93c0ca-42e8-5d66-981a-2e80551e5f21" "answer-b427e1ff-6b3d-5b12-b7a7-f23687f41949" "answer-1dba2a64-ae52-5c4f-bd51-5ef13be3cb6d" "answer-5730d517-7c69-5980-b411-3b3dad664998"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-94d307ae-d9c4-5198-958c-581b3e680cb8" :answer/id #uuid "94d307ae-d9c4-5198-958c-581b3e680cb8" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-4b93c0ca-42e8-5d66-981a-2e80551e5f21" :answer/id #uuid "4b93c0ca-42e8-5d66-981a-2e80551e5f21" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-b427e1ff-6b3d-5b12-b7a7-f23687f41949" :answer/id #uuid "b427e1ff-6b3d-5b12-b7a7-f23687f41949" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "1.5" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-1dba2a64-ae52-5c4f-bd51-5ef13be3cb6d" :answer/id #uuid "1dba2a64-ae52-5c4f-bd51-5ef13be3cb6d" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-5730d517-7c69-5980-b411-3b3dad664998" :answer/id #uuid "5730d517-7c69-5980-b411-3b3dad664998" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "3" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-931e8538-9965-59c5-8971-be8d18bbaef6" :question/id #uuid "931e8538-9965-59c5-8971-be8d18bbaef6" :question/math-academy-id "q-163837" :question/problem "![](images/08/081dab1fcd5ccde5281ec4369417c4b0911a404695e9d147b046daf68f8621a6.png)\n\nThe graph of the function $z=f(x,y)$ is shown above.\nWhat is the global minimum of the function?" :question/worked-solution "First, we recall the following definitions:\n\n- The global minimum of a function is the smallest value it attains on its domain. There can only be (at most) one global minimum.\n\n- A global minimum point is a point $(x,y,z)$ where a function reaches a global minimum. There can be zero, one, or many global minimum points.\n\nFor our function, the minimum value reached by the function is $f(x,y)=-5.$ Therefore, the global minimum is $-5.$ The function reaches this value at   $(x,y)=(0,3).$\n\n![](images/c1/c1fb4d47cc10c74537e01803a633fd9f8d6c9b1c6f8b8cd945bfba058861c2d1.png)" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-48c4ceca-3df8-5383-9289-174b40f5a7c6"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-48c4ceca-3df8-5383-9289-174b40f5a7c6" :answer-field/id #uuid "48c4ceca-3df8-5383-9289-174b40f5a7c6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-c821506c-1b26-5aad-87f9-efd4a40384ae" :answer-field/choices ["answer-8c693c22-c800-52a4-9e88-159bdde7ac6a" "answer-880d681f-4e87-56e3-b724-e08426dfd46e" "answer-41662308-758c-57f7-94d5-3b0fe16cf47b" "answer-c821506c-1b26-5aad-87f9-efd4a40384ae" "answer-ce3551a0-fc31-5428-8862-90ab0efeebcc"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-8c693c22-c800-52a4-9e88-159bdde7ac6a" :answer/id #uuid "8c693c22-c800-52a4-9e88-159bdde7ac6a" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-2" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-880d681f-4e87-56e3-b724-e08426dfd46e" :answer/id #uuid "880d681f-4e87-56e3-b724-e08426dfd46e" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-3" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-41662308-758c-57f7-94d5-3b0fe16cf47b" :answer/id #uuid "41662308-758c-57f7-94d5-3b0fe16cf47b" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-4" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-c821506c-1b26-5aad-87f9-efd4a40384ae" :answer/id #uuid "c821506c-1b26-5aad-87f9-efd4a40384ae" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-5" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-ce3551a0-fc31-5428-8862-90ab0efeebcc" :answer/id #uuid "ce3551a0-fc31-5428-8862-90ab0efeebcc" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-e9cc186b-22af-55d8-bf89-1dd19ae7b90f" :question/id #uuid "e9cc186b-22af-55d8-bf89-1dd19ae7b90f" :question/math-academy-id "q-164154" :question/problem "![](images/81/81604581fae8b25776727eca4450caa7d48c42a781706455ed585d5cfc4a25fa.png)\n\nThe graph of the function $z=f(x,y)$ is shown above. Which of the points shown are local maximum points?" :question/worked-solution "A function $f(x,y)$ has a local maximum point at $(a,b)$ if $f(x,y)\\le f(a,b)$ for all points $(x,y)$ in a neighborhood of $(a,b).$ \n\nIn other words, we can take a neighborhood of $(x,y)$-values such that the point $(x,y,f(x,y))$ is the highest point on the graph in the neighborhood.\n\nSuch regions exist for the points $A$ and $C.$ Hence $A$ and $C$ are local maximum points.\n\n**Note**: For the point $A({x}_{A},{y}_{A},{z}_{A}),$ there are points $(x,y)$ in the surrounding regions such that $f({x}_{A},{y}_{A})=f(x,y).$ However, since the definition of local extrema is non-strict, $f(x,y)\\le f(a,b)$, the point $A$ does satisfy the conditions for a local maximum.\n\n![](images/36/3651b43178819666002775a26071a0bd2c9cb48b58a3c5516c38e085d50fc4a6.png)" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-4706282d-9a32-5f14-a57a-fcb85720d9bf"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-4706282d-9a32-5f14-a57a-fcb85720d9bf" :answer-field/id #uuid "4706282d-9a32-5f14-a57a-fcb85720d9bf" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-e36fc252-161e-5249-b04c-21f5ae164898" :answer-field/choices ["answer-e36fc252-161e-5249-b04c-21f5ae164898" "answer-8e775825-6245-5d2d-930c-aa1a6446fcc5" "answer-d3fc7d81-3c2e-547e-b4b6-9d3a5644b96e" "answer-e27f6f79-0b4c-502b-ae85-1bf343f859a8" "answer-1d1effde-74f3-572c-895a-c689fd4d6886"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-e36fc252-161e-5249-b04c-21f5ae164898" :answer/id #uuid "e36fc252-161e-5249-b04c-21f5ae164898" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$A$ and $C$ only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-8e775825-6245-5d2d-930c-aa1a6446fcc5" :answer/id #uuid "8e775825-6245-5d2d-930c-aa1a6446fcc5" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$A$ only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-d3fc7d81-3c2e-547e-b4b6-9d3a5644b96e" :answer/id #uuid "d3fc7d81-3c2e-547e-b4b6-9d3a5644b96e" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$B$ and $C$ only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-e27f6f79-0b4c-502b-ae85-1bf343f859a8" :answer/id #uuid "e27f6f79-0b4c-502b-ae85-1bf343f859a8" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$C$ and $D$ only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-1d1effde-74f3-572c-895a-c689fd4d6886" :answer/id #uuid "1d1effde-74f3-572c-895a-c689fd4d6886" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$C$ only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-73e92678-31f3-525b-9096-a06391739cab" :question/id #uuid "73e92678-31f3-525b-9096-a06391739cab" :question/math-academy-id "q-164151" :question/problem "![](images/f7/f7429a2fc291fc9e14685208288f670cca341a260ac13b358c52d99c35e13b4f.png)\n\nThe graph of the function $z=f(x,y)$ is shown above. Which of the points shown are local minimum points?" :question/worked-solution "A function $f(x,y)$ has a local minimum point at $(a,b)$ if $f(x,y)\\ge f(a,b)$ for all points $(x,y)$ in a neighborhood of $(a,b).$ \n\nIn other words, we can take a neighborhood of $(x,y)$-values such that the point $(x,y,f(x,y))$ is the lowest point on the graph in the neighborhood.\n\nSuch neighborhoods exist for the points $A$ and $D.$ Hence, $A$ and $D$ are local minima.\n\n![](images/b8/b8952aa59c9351c9723cd62f35073d46d7ddfd46afa9a9b3ddae7ce149065c59.png)" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-caaf0180-b775-51a5-8357-5dbad212c145"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-caaf0180-b775-51a5-8357-5dbad212c145" :answer-field/id #uuid "caaf0180-b775-51a5-8357-5dbad212c145" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-671887d0-2723-5a15-b0f2-b1adf3728767" :answer-field/choices ["answer-671887d0-2723-5a15-b0f2-b1adf3728767" "answer-5d6a8563-51e7-5f75-b1f9-5030c16e40a1" "answer-7ec46caa-3ab4-5a82-8cef-51349b889f2b" "answer-c579e011-3bca-5b23-92a7-a4c2a45142bc" "answer-7eb013d8-4bec-59b6-be78-16c6b4278be3"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-671887d0-2723-5a15-b0f2-b1adf3728767" :answer/id #uuid "671887d0-2723-5a15-b0f2-b1adf3728767" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$A$ and $D$ only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-5d6a8563-51e7-5f75-b1f9-5030c16e40a1" :answer/id #uuid "5d6a8563-51e7-5f75-b1f9-5030c16e40a1" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$A$ only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-7ec46caa-3ab4-5a82-8cef-51349b889f2b" :answer/id #uuid "7ec46caa-3ab4-5a82-8cef-51349b889f2b" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$B$ only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-c579e011-3bca-5b23-92a7-a4c2a45142bc" :answer/id #uuid "c579e011-3bca-5b23-92a7-a4c2a45142bc" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$C$ and $D$ only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-7eb013d8-4bec-59b6-be78-16c6b4278be3" :answer/id #uuid "7eb013d8-4bec-59b6-be78-16c6b4278be3" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "The function does not have local minima" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-7f6e52f3-8502-516e-a6ab-c65771746796" :question/id #uuid "7f6e52f3-8502-516e-a6ab-c65771746796" :question/math-academy-id "q-164168" :question/problem "![](images/f3/f3852805b0ae44433828113396fa63d0688ec06ae57886428815515e521baa49.png)\n\nThe graph of the function $z=f(x,y)$ is shown above. Which of the points shown are local minimum points?" :question/worked-solution "A function $f(x,y)$ has a local minimum point at $(a,b)$ if $f(x,y)\\ge f(a,b)$ for all points $(x,y)$ in a neighborhood of $(a,b).$ \n\nIn other words, we can take a neighborhood of $(x,y)$-values such that the point $(x,y,f(x,y))$ is the lowest point on the graph in the neighborhood.\n\nSuch regions exist for the points $B$ and $D.$ Hence, $B$ and $D$ are local minimum points.\n\n**Note**: For the point $D({x}_{D},{y}_{D},{z}_{D}),$ there are points $(x,y)$ in the surrounding regions such that $f({x}_{D},{y}_{D})=f(x,y).$ However, since the definition of local extrema is non-strict, $f(x,y)\\ge f(a,b)$, the point $D$ does satisfy the conditions for a local minimum.\n\n![](images/f1/f10742ca92d2429c35b36cf77ae2d857774faba848a74291bbfa8e5ac71eeada.png)" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-dd8d1baf-33b0-5256-8cc7-c9e816543e66"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-dd8d1baf-33b0-5256-8cc7-c9e816543e66" :answer-field/id #uuid "dd8d1baf-33b0-5256-8cc7-c9e816543e