borhood of $(x,y)$-values such that the point $(x,y,f(x,y))$ is the lowest point on the graph in the neighborhood.\n\nSuch neighborhoods exist for the points $A$ and $C.$ Hence, $A$ and $C$ are local minima.\n\n![](images/02/0262a775dad79697ea888b6047e607c12ec804dbb734b72b2ca8fda54d6c130f.png)" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-ddd2fb64-7d5d-5d61-9a82-d15eb97782df"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-ddd2fb64-7d5d-5d61-9a82-d15eb97782df" :answer-field/id #uuid "ddd2fb64-7d5d-5d61-9a82-d15eb97782df" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-479b6949-d768-5a91-943c-b362a5bb5ece" :answer-field/choices ["answer-479b6949-d768-5a91-943c-b362a5bb5ece" "answer-b5f6e322-f58a-582d-ac9a-02e88741ca94" "answer-9e9c4318-1e04-56fd-b1b0-d959a8de76d3" "answer-9f9af5d3-d4aa-5e2f-8e3a-f1745f0df43d" "answer-8c979729-0c50-52fc-93ef-b13c63005c88"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-479b6949-d768-5a91-943c-b362a5bb5ece" :answer/id #uuid "479b6949-d768-5a91-943c-b362a5bb5ece" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$A$ and $C$ only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-b5f6e322-f58a-582d-ac9a-02e88741ca94" :answer/id #uuid "b5f6e322-f58a-582d-ac9a-02e88741ca94" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$A$ only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-9e9c4318-1e04-56fd-b1b0-d959a8de76d3" :answer/id #uuid "9e9c4318-1e04-56fd-b1b0-d959a8de76d3" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$A,$ $B,$ and $C$ only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-9f9af5d3-d4aa-5e2f-8e3a-f1745f0df43d" :answer/id #uuid "9f9af5d3-d4aa-5e2f-8e3a-f1745f0df43d" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$A,$ $C,$ and $D$ only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-8c979729-0c50-52fc-93ef-b13c63005c88" :answer/id #uuid "8c979729-0c50-52fc-93ef-b13c63005c88" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$C$ only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-c719ba47-2cb2-55e7-85ae-fa0a3ade984e" :question/id #uuid "c719ba47-2cb2-55e7-85ae-fa0a3ade984e" :question/math-academy-id "q-164153" :question/problem "![](images/79/79971602dd6834147c3db9361c94853a5a66c8e899978df3629d2841326fe62c.png)\n\nThe graph of the function $z=f(x,y)$ is shown above. Which of the points shown are local maximum points?" :question/worked-solution "A function $f(x,y)$ has a local maximum point at $(a,b)$ if $f(x,y)\\le f(a,b)$ for all points $(x,y)$ in a neighborhood of $(a,b).$ \n\nIn other words, we can take a neighborhood of $(x,y)$-values such that the point $(x,y,f(x,y))$ is the highest point on the graph in the neighborhood.\n\nSuch neighborhood exists for the point $B.$ Hence, $B$ is a local maximum.\n\n![](images/68/685c03b86db93032c8a79334c5fa46c561dd263a9aa4f52b9d7e6f41c3bb827c.png)" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-2bbf6504-629f-56a6-8d02-ea8b2cdc4409"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-2bbf6504-629f-56a6-8d02-ea8b2cdc4409" :answer-field/id #uuid "2bbf6504-629f-56a6-8d02-ea8b2cdc4409" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-3674a427-b1f6-5423-9cad-a53248cc1526" :answer-field/choices ["answer-2683d2d1-d298-565b-a7c7-9a4e0ec1b79e" "answer-867414de-477d-5e96-ac86-a8231e8e612f" "answer-3674a427-b1f6-5423-9cad-a53248cc1526" "answer-3af27a0e-03ce-5737-81df-4b4e8b4ce792" "answer-ecbd6041-7dad-5acb-839a-6742c3614749"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-2683d2d1-d298-565b-a7c7-9a4e0ec1b79e" :answer/id #uuid "2683d2d1-d298-565b-a7c7-9a4e0ec1b79e" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$A$ and $B$ only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-867414de-477d-5e96-ac86-a8231e8e612f" :answer/id #uuid "867414de-477d-5e96-ac86-a8231e8e612f" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$A$ only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-3674a427-b1f6-5423-9cad-a53248cc1526" :answer/id #uuid "3674a427-b1f6-5423-9cad-a53248cc1526" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$B$ only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-3af27a0e-03ce-5737-81df-4b4e8b4ce792" :answer/id #uuid "3af27a0e-03ce-5737-81df-4b4e8b4ce792" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$C$ and $D$ only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-ecbd6041-7dad-5acb-839a-6742c3614749" :answer/id #uuid "ecbd6041-7dad-5acb-839a-6742c3614749" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "The function does not have local maxima" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-463ee037-d37e-5536-bfb1-df9c529010e2" :question/id #uuid "463ee037-d37e-5536-bfb1-df9c529010e2" :question/math-academy-id "q-163776" :question/problem "The quadratic Taylor approximation of the function $f(x)$ about $x=a\\in {R}^{2}$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x)≈1+\\frac{1}{2}(x-a)^{T}[\\begin{aligned}3 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{aligned}](x-a).\n$$\n\nGiven that the eigenvalues of ${f}^{″}(a)$ are ${\\lambda }_{1}=1-\\sqrt{5}$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=1+\\sqrt{5},$ what type of point is $a?$" :question/worked-solution "The point $a\\in {R}^{n}$ is a critical point of the function $f:{R}^{n}\\to R$ if ${f}^{′}(a)={0}^{T}.$ In such cases, we can classify $a$ as follows:\n\n- if ${f}^{″}(a)$ is positive definite, then $a$ is a local minimum\n\n- if ${f}^{″}(a)$ is negative definite, then $a$ is a local maximum\n\n- if ${f}^{″}(a)$ is neither positive-definite nor negative-definite, then $a$ is a saddle point\n\nThe quadratic Taylor expansion of a function $f:{R}^{n}\\to R$ about $x=a\\in {R}^{n}$ given by\n\n$$\nf(x)≈f(a)+{f}^{′}(a)(x-a)+\\frac{1}{2}(x-a)^{T}{f}^{″}(a)(x-a).\n$$\n\nSince the quadratic Taylor expansion of $f$ has no $(x-a)$ term, we can conclude that ${f}^{′}(a)={0}^{T}.$ Hence, $a$ is a critical point of $f.$\n\nFinally, notice that ${f}^{″}(a)$ has both negative and positive eigenvalues:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }_{1} & =1-\\sqrt{5}<0 \\\\ {\\lambda }_{2} & =1+\\sqrt{5}>0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the matrix is neither positive-definite nor negative-definite. Therefore, $a$ is a saddle point of $f.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-812379f4-7ca2-54a9-9137-3e6202ac33a8"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-812379f4-7ca2-54a9-9137-3e6202ac33a8" :answer-field/id #uuid "812379f4-7ca2-54a9-9137-3e6202ac33a8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-ee820c78-33c5-521b-97ae-0bef262419cd" :answer-field/choices ["answer-9984c603-9561-5d15-a7da-151b72f56966" "answer-48aa8c46-39c6-5468-85c5-ec68e86acbdd" "answer-d563919d-d0e0-5990-917f-9b961dfd1ad5" "answer-63a1c505-6f99-5e6b-92df-f1c0f1d2c71d" "answer-ee820c78-33c5-521b-97ae-0bef262419cd"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-9984c603-9561-5d15-a7da-151b72f56966" :answer/id #uuid "9984c603-9561-5d15-a7da-151b72f56966" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "inconclusive" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-48aa8c46-39c6-5468-85c5-ec68e86acbdd" :answer/id #uuid "48aa8c46-39c6-5468-85c5-ec68e86acbdd" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "local maximum" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-d563919d-d0e0-5990-917f-9b961dfd1ad5" :answer/id #uuid "d563919d-d0e0-5990-917f-9b961dfd1ad5" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "local minimum" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-63a1c505-6f99-5e6b-92df-f1c0f1d2c71d" :answer/id #uuid "63a1c505-6f99-5e6b-92df-f1c0f1d2c71d" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "not a critical point" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-ee820c78-33c5-521b-97ae-0bef262419cd" :answer/id #uuid "ee820c78-33c5-521b-97ae-0bef262419cd" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "saddle point" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186124820 :question/problem "The quadratic Taylor approximation of the function $f(x)$ about $x=a\\in {R}^{2}$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x)≈-1+\\frac{1}{2}(x-a)^{T}[\\begin{aligned}4 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 2\\end{aligned}](x-a).\n$$\n\nGiven that the eigenvalues of ${f}^{″}(a)$ are ${\\lambda }_{1}=3-\\sqrt{5}$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=3+\\sqrt{5},$ what type of point is $a?$" :question/worked-solution "The point $a\\in {R}^{n}$ is a critical point of the function $f:{R}^{n}\\to R$ if ${f}^{′}(a)={0}^{T}.$ In such cases, we can classify $a$ as follows:\n\n- if ${f}^{″}(a)$ is positive definite, then $a$ is a local minimum\n\n- if ${f}^{″}(a)$ is negative definite, then $a$ is a local maximum\n\n- if ${f}^{″}(a)$ is neither positive-definite nor negative-definite, then $a$ is a saddle point\n\nThe quadratic Taylor expansion of a function $f:{R}^{n}\\to R$ about $x=a\\in {R}^{n}$ given by\n\n$$\nf(x)≈f(a)+{f}^{′}(a)(x-a)+\\frac{1}{2}(x-a)^{T}{f}^{″}(a)(x-a).\n$$\n\nSince the quadratic Taylor expansion of $f$ has no $(x-a)$ term, we can conclude that ${f}^{′}(a)={0}^{T}.$ Hence, $a$ is a critical point of $f.$\n\nFinally, notice that both the eigenvalues of ${f}^{″}(a)$ are positive:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }_{1} & =3-\\sqrt{5}>0 \\\\ {\\lambda }_{2} & =3+\\sqrt{5}>0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the matrix is positive definite. Therefore, $a$ is a local minimum of $f.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186121517 :answer-field/correct 17592186121522 :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "question-4c7c764b-5efc-585f-b1d6-ab32ef1c26ef" :question/id #uuid "4c7c764b-5efc-585f-b1d6-ab32ef1c26ef" :question/math-academy-id "q-163772" :question/problem "The quadratic Taylor approximation of the function $f(x)$ about $x=a\\in {R}^{2}$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x)≈-\\frac{1}{3}+\\frac{1}{2}(x-a)^{T}[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{aligned}](x-a).\n$$\n\nGiven that the eigenvalues of ${f}^{″}(a)$ are ${\\lambda }_{1}=1$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=3,$ what type of point is $a?$" :question/worked-solution "The point $a\\in {R}^{n}$ is a critical point of the function $f:{R}^{n}\\to R$ if ${f}^{′}(a)={0}^{T}.$ In such cases, we can classify $a$ as follows:\n\n- if ${f}^{″}(a)$ is positive definite, then $a$ is a local minimum\n\n- if ${f}^{″}(a)$ is negative definite, then $a$ is a local maximum\n\n- if ${f}^{″}(a)$ is neither positive-definite nor negative-definite, then $a$ is a saddle point\n\nThe quadratic Taylor expansion of a function $f:{R}^{n}\\to R$ about $x=a\\in {R}^{n}$ given by\n\n$$\nf(x)≈f(a)+{f}^{′}(a)(x-a)+\\frac{1}{2}(x-a)^{T}{f}^{″}(a)(x-a).\n$$\n\nSince the quadratic Taylor expansion of $f$ has no $(x-a)$ term, we can conclude that ${f}^{′}(a)={0}^{T}.$ Hence, $a$ is a critical point of $f.$\n\nFinally, notice that both the eigenvalues of ${f}^{″}(a)$ are positive:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }_{1} & =1>0 \\\\ {\\lambda }_{2} & =3>0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the matrix is positive definite. Therefore, $a$ is a local minimum of $f.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-6988e8b1-324d-5035-b234-84ec660debef"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-6988e8b1-324d-5035-b234-84ec660debef" :answer-field/id #uuid "6988e8b1-324d-5035-b234-84ec660debef" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-270cb157-312b-598a-a963-8332f3594021" :answer-field/choices ["answer-5d10f4aa-be44-5d96-9b85-bf9265aaedd3" "answer-66cf0a35-8b8a-52ab-82bc-4a4518a010c3" "answer-270cb157-312b-598a-a963-8332f3594021" "answer-cd819cdb-57a3-5138-b681-8a96c46bbbb7" "answer-a3ac3574-a248-5137-9dca-b1453562f8cd"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-5d10f4aa-be44-5d96-9b85-bf9265aaedd3" :answer/id #uuid "5d10f4aa-be44-5d96-9b85-bf9265aaedd3" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "inconclusive" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-66cf0a35-8b8a-52ab-82bc-4a4518a010c3" :answer/id #uuid "66cf0a35-8b8a-52ab-82bc-4a4518a010c3" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "local maximum" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-270cb157-312b-598a-a963-8332f3594021" :answer/id #uuid "270cb157-312b-598a-a963-8332f3594021" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "local minimum" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-cd819cdb-57a3-5138-b681-8a96c46bbbb7" :answer/id #uuid "cd819cdb-57a3-5138-b681-8a96c46bbbb7" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "not a critical point" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-a3ac3574-a248-5137-9dca-b1453562f8cd" :answer/id #uuid "a3ac3574-a248-5137-9dca-b1453562f8cd" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "saddle point" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-2a2b77d8-21b3-512a-8f60-975fa8c9b29e" :question/id #uuid "2a2b77d8-21b3-512a-8f60-975fa8c9b29e" :question/math-academy-id "q-163783" :question/problem "The quadratic Taylor approximation of the function $f(x)$ about $x=a\\in {R}^{3}$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x)≈-78+\\frac{1}{2}(x-a)^{T}[\\begin{aligned}-4 & 0 & 4 \\\\ 0 & -6 & 0 \\\\ 4 & 0 & 8\\end{aligned}](x-a).\n$$\n\nGiven that the eigenvalues of ${f}^{″}(a)$ are ${\\lambda }_{1}=-6,{\\lambda }_{2}=2-2\\sqrt{13}$ and ${\\lambda }_{3}=2+2\\sqrt{13},$ what type of point is $a?$" :question/worked-solution "The point $a\\in {R}^{n}$ is a critical point of the function $f:{R}^{n}\\to R$ if ${f}^{′}(a)={0}^{T}.$ In such cases, we can classify $a$ as follows:\n\n- if ${f}^{″}(a)$ is positive definite, then $a$ is a local minimum\n\n- if ${f}^{″}(a)$ is negative definite, then $a$ is a local maximum\n\n- if ${f}^{″}(a)$ is neither positive-definite nor negative-definite, then $a$ is a saddle point\n\nThe quadratic Taylor expansion of a function $f:{R}^{n}\\to R$ about $x=a\\in {R}^{n}$ given by\n\n$$\nf(x)≈f(a)+{f}^{′}(a)(x-a)+\\frac{1}{2}(x-a)^{T}{f}^{″}(a)(x-a).\n$$\n\nSince the quadratic Taylor expansion of $f$ has no $(x-a)$ term, we can conclude that ${f}^{′}(a)={0}^{T}.$ Hence, $a$ is a critical point of $f.$\n\nFinally, notice that ${f}^{″}(a)$ has both positive and negative eigenvalues:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }_{1} & =-6<0 \\\\ {\\lambda }_{2} & =2-2\\sqrt{13}<0 \\\\ {\\lambda }_{3} & =2+2\\sqrt{13}>0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the matrix is neither positive-definite nor negative-definite. Therefore, $a$ is a saddle point of $f.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-a84e77c5-ac0f-5d82-92c5-daa66014b1bc"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-a84e77c5-ac0f-5d82-92c5-daa66014b1bc" :answer-field/id #uuid "a84e77c5-ac0f-5d82-92c5-daa66014b1bc" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-ec57deac-58c3-5209-82ba-5d99ae0db2b0" :answer-field/choices ["answer-77644fed-85aa-50e4-ad64-f4f0dfaa2ac1" "answer-6dd13874-5f64-5852-a430-9a9d7ca920b8" "answer-1342b725-dc31-5b5a-99d1-77316495d1ef" "answer-42eede05-2998-52b8-82a7-f2c9485a49f0" "answer-ec57deac-58c3-5209-82ba-5d99ae0db2b0"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-77644fed-85aa-50e4-ad64-f4f0dfaa2ac1" :answer/id #uuid "77644fed-85aa-50e4-ad64-f4f0dfaa2ac1" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "inconclusive" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-6dd13874-5f64-5852-a430-9a9d7ca920b8" :answer/id #uuid "6dd13874-5f64-5852-a430-9a9d7ca920b8" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "local maximum" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-1342b725-dc31-5b5a-99d1-77316495d1ef" :answer/id #uuid "1342b725-dc31-5b5a-99d1-77316495d1ef" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "local minimum" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-42eede05-2998-52b8-82a7-f2c9485a49f0" :answer/id #uuid "42eede05-2998-52b8-82a7-f2c9485a49f0" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "not a critical point" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-ec57deac-58c3-5209-82ba-5d99ae0db2b0" :answer/id #uuid "ec57deac-58c3-5209-82ba-5d99ae0db2b0" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "saddle point" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-ae219904-d456-5249-bf4b-05599f3e75b5" :question/id #uuid "ae219904-d456-5249-bf4b-05599f3e75b5" :question/math-academy-id "q-163774" :question/problem "The quadratic Taylor approximation of the function $f(x)$ about $x=a\\in {R}^{2}$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x)≈2+\\frac{1}{2}(x-a)^{T}[\\begin{aligned}4 & -1 \\\\ -1 & -6\\end{aligned}](x-a).\n$$\n\nGiven that the eigenvalues of ${f}^{″}(a)$ are ${\\lambda }_{1}=-1+\\sqrt{26}$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=-1-\\sqrt{26},$ what type of point is $a?$" :question/worked-solution "The point $a\\in {R}^{n}$ is a critical point of the function $f:{R}^{n}\\to R$ if ${f}^{′}(a)={0}^{T}.$ In such cases, we can classify $a$ as follows:\n\n- if ${f}^{″}(a)$ is positive definite, then $a$ is a local minimum\n\n- if ${f}^{″}(a)$ is negative definite, then $a$ is a local maximum\n\n- if ${f}^{″}(a)$ is neither positive-definite nor negative-definite, then $a$ is a saddle point\n\nThe quadratic Taylor expansion of a function $f:{R}^{n}\\to R$ about $x=a\\in {R}^{n}$ given by\n\n$$\nf(x)≈f(a)+{f}^{′}(a)(x-a)+\\frac{1}{2}(x-a)^{T}{f}^{″}(a)(x-a).\n$$\n\nSince the quadratic Taylor expansion of $f$ has no $(x-a)$ term, we can conclude that ${f}^{′}(a)={0}^{T}.$ Hence, $a$ is a critical point of $f.$\n\nFinally, notice that ${f}^{″}(a)$ has both negative and positive eigenvalues:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }_{1} & =-1+\\sqrt{26}>0 \\\\ {\\lambda }_{2} & =-1-\\sqrt{26}<0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the matrix is neither positive-definite nor negative-definite. Therefore, $a$ is a saddle point of $f.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-d9aa5f48-03a4-5e22-bfab-3a430f54be65"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-d9aa5f48-03a4-5e22-bfab-3a430f54be65" :answer-field/id #uuid "d9aa5f48-03a4-5e22-bfab-3a430f54be65" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-002ed759-21f8-5564-aa23-e5ae38d108b5" :answer-field/choices ["answer-ada293be-8d8f-53b2-8e22-8254f87db838" "answer-fbc3a5a8-400b-5371-8e6e-5ad2e09e9946" "answer-7bf6cd21-6e6c-5f23-90a9-561d369a6b59" "answer-f1ef5c12-e171-59db-9f19-75d60d6fc0d1" "answer-002ed759-21f8-5564-aa23-e5ae38d108b5"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-ada293be-8d8f-53b2-8e22-8254f87db838" :answer/id #uuid "ada293be-8d8f-53b2-8e22-8254f87db838" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "inconclusive" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-fbc3a5a8-400b-5371-8e6e-5ad2e09e9946" :answer/id #uuid "fbc3a5a8-400b-5371-8e6e-5ad2e09e9946" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "local maximum" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-7bf6cd21-6e6c-5f23-90a9-561d369a6b59" :answer/id #uuid "7bf6cd21-6e6c-5f23-90a9-561d369a6b59" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "local minimum" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-f1ef5c12-e171-59db-9f19-75d60d6fc0d1" :answer/id #uuid "f1ef5c12-e171-59db-9f19-75d60d6fc0d1" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "not a critical point" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-002ed759-21f8-5564-aa23-e5ae38d108b5" :answer/id #uuid "002ed759-21f8-5564-aa23-e5ae38d108b5" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "saddle point" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-65b2f5b4-89e0-551f-a08f-1b697ac55d76" :question/id #uuid "65b2f5b4-89e0-551f-a08f-1b697ac55d76" :question/math-academy-id "q-163786" :question/problem "The quadratic Taylor approximation of the function $f(x)$ about $x=a\\in {R}^{3}$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x)≈-2+\\frac{1}{2}(x-a)^{T}A(x-a),\n$$\n\nwhere \n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 1 & 3\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n \n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\nI. ${a}_{11}=2$ and $det(A)=3$\n\nII. $A$ is neither positive-definite nor negative-definite\n\nIII. $a$ is a saddle point of $f$" :question/worked-solution "The point $a\\in {R}^{n}$ is a critical point of the function $f:{R}^{n}\\to R$ if ${f}^{′}(a)={0}^{T}.$ In such cases, we can classify $a$ as follows:\n\n- if ${f}^{″}(a)$ is positive definite, then $a$ is a local minimum\n\n- if ${f}^{″}(a)$ is negative definite, then $a$ is a local maximum\n\n- if ${f}^{″}(a)$ is neither positive-definite nor negative-definite, then $a$ is a saddle point\n\nIn this case, we have $A={f}^{″}(a).$ Now, before we examine the statements, we recall how to categorize quadratic forms.\n\nLet ${Δ}_{i}$ for $i=1,2,…,n$  denote the leading principal minors of an $n\\times n$ matrix $A.$ Then, according to Sylvester's criterion, the quadratic form ${x}^{T}\\,A\\,x$ (as well as the matrix $A$) is \n\n- positive-definite if and only if ${Δ}_{i}>0$ for all $i=1,2,…,n,$\n\n- negative-definite if and only if, for all $i=1,2,…,n,$ we have ${Δ}_{i}<0$ when $i$ is odd, and ${Δ}_{i}>0$ when $i$ is even.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, ${a}_{11}=2$ and\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(A) & =|\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 1 & 3\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =2\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 3\\end{aligned}|+(-1)\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =2(2)-1(1) \\\\  & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is false. Notice the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Δ}_{1} & ={a}_{11}=2>0 \\\\ {Δ}_{2} & =|\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}|=2>0 \\\\ {Δ}_{3} & =|A|=3>0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, by Sylvester's criterion, the matrix $A$ is positive-definite.\n\n- Statement III is false. Since the matrix $A$ is positive-definite, the critical point $a$ is a local minimum of $f.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-547254ce-982e-5bb1-87b7-5c56ae4ce713"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-547254ce-982e-5bb1-87b7-5c56ae4ce713" :answer-field/id #uuid "547254ce-982e-5bb1-87b7-5c56ae4ce713" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-2d723f1a-3e00-5451-9a8e-3979cfbee414" :answer-field/choices ["answer-2d723f1a-3e00-5451-9a8e-3979cfbee414" "answer-65b7c2cb-305e-5629-b5ca-a76ce120a41c" "answer-cb29e13c-7941-5abb-8dab-e750fa074891" "answer-439fd18d-a45e-5a4a-9276-7284619940e3" "answer-b4871234-59ca-5a03-86ce-df6b280edd92"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-2d723f1a-3e00-5451-9a8e-3979cfbee414" :answer/id #uuid "2d723f1a-3e00-5451-9a8e-3979cfbee414" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-65b7c2cb-305e-5629-b5ca-a76ce120a41c" :answer/id #uuid "65b7c2cb-305e-5629-b5ca-a76ce120a41c" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-cb29e13c-7941-5abb-8dab-e750fa074891" :answer/id #uuid "cb29e13c-7941-5abb-8dab-e750fa074891" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II and III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-439fd18d-a45e-5a4a-9276-7284619940e3" :answer/id #uuid "439fd18d-a45e-5a4a-9276-7284619940e3" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-b4871234-59ca-5a03-86ce-df6b280edd92" :answer/id #uuid "b4871234-59ca-5a03-86ce-df6b280edd92" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "None of the statements are true" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186124821 :question/problem "The quadratic Taylor approximation of the function $f(x)$ about $x=a\\in {R}^{2}$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x)≈-12+\\frac{1}{2}(x-a)^{T}A(x-a),\n$$\n\nwhere \n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 4 \\\\ 4 & 4\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n \n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\nI. ${a}_{11}=-2$ and $det(A)=-24$\n\nII. $A$ is positive-definite\n\nIII. $a$ is a local minimum of $f$" :question/worked-solution "The point $a\\in {R}^{n}$ is a critical point of the function $f:{R}^{n}\\to R$ if ${f}^{′}(a)={0}^{T}.$ In such cases, we can classify $a$ as follows:\n\n- if ${f}^{″}(a)$ is positive definite, then $a$ is a local minimum\n\n- if ${f}^{″}(a)$ is negative definite, then $a$ is a local maximum\n\n- if ${f}^{″}(a)$ is neither positive-definite nor negative-definite, then $a$ is a saddle point\n\nIn this case, we have $A={f}^{″}(a).$ Now, before we examine the statements, let's recall how to categorize quadratic forms.\n\nLet ${Δ}_{i}$ for $i=1,2,…,n$  denote the leading principal minors of an $n\\times n$ matrix $A.$ Then, according to Sylvester's criterion, the quadratic form ${x}^{T}\\,A\\,x$ (as well as the matrix $A$) is \n\n- positive-definite if and only if ${Δ}_{i}>0$ for all $i=1,2,…,n,$\n\n- negative-definite if and only if, for all $i=1,2,…,n,$ we have ${Δ}_{i}<0$ when $i$ is odd, and ${Δ}_{i}>0$ when $i$ is even.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, ${a}_{11}=-2$ and\n\n$$\ndet(A)=|\\begin{aligned}-2 & 4 \\\\ 4 & 4\\end{aligned}|=-24.\n$$\n\n- Statement II is false. Notice that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Δ}_{1} & ={a}_{11}=-2<0, \\\\ {Δ}_{2} & =|\\begin{aligned}-2 & 4 \\\\ 4 & 4\\end{aligned}|=-24<0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, by Sylvester's criterion, the matrix $A$ is neither positive-definite nor negative-definite.\n\n- Statement III is false. Since the matrix $A$ is neither positive-definite nor negative-definite, the critical point $a$ is a saddle point of $f.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186121523 :answer-field/correct 17592186121525 :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "question-a6771fc8-6834-5ff6-940e-46f1464b61f6" :question/id #uuid "a6771fc8-6834-5ff6-940e-46f1464b61f6" :question/math-academy-id "q-163778" :question/problem "The quadratic Taylor approximation of the function $f(x)$ about $x=a\\in {R}^{2}$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x)≈-1+\\frac{1}{2}(x-a)^{T}A(x-a),\n$$\n\nwhere \n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 5\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n \n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\nI. ${a}_{11}=1$ and $det(A)=4$\n\nII. $A$ is positive-definite\n\nIII. $a$ is a local maximum of $f$" :question/worked-solution "The point $a\\in {R}^{n}$ is a critical point of the function $f:{R}^{n}\\to R$ if ${f}^{′}(a)={0}^{T}.$ In such cases, we can classify $a$ as follows:\n\n- if ${f}^{″}(a)$ is positive definite, then $a$ is a local minimum\n\n- if ${f}^{″}(a)$ is negative definite, then $a$ is a local maximum\n\n- if ${f}^{″}(a)$ is neither positive-definite nor negative-definite, then $a$ is a saddle point\n\nIn this case, we have $A={f}^{″}(a).$ Now, before we examine the statements, let's recall how to categorize quadratic forms.\n\nLet ${Δ}_{i}$ for $i=1,2,…,n$  denote the leading principal minors of an $n\\times n$ matrix $A.$ Then, according to Sylvester's criterion, the quadratic form ${x}^{T}\\,A\\,x$ (as well as the matrix $A$) is \n\n- positive-definite if and only if ${Δ}_{i}>0$ for all $i=1,2,…,n,$\n\n- negative-definite if and only if, for all $i=1,2,…,n,$ we have ${Δ}_{i}<0$ when $i$ is odd, and ${Δ}_{i}>0$ when $i$ is even.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, ${a}_{11}=1$ and\n\n$$\ndet(A)=|\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 5\\end{aligned}|=4.\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true. Notice that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Δ}_{1} & ={a}_{11}=1>0, \\\\ {Δ}_{2} & =|\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 5\\end{aligned}|=4>0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, by Sylvester's criterion, the matrix $A$ is positive-definite.\n\n- Statement III is false. Since the matrix $A$ is positive-definite, the critical point $a$ is a local minimum of $f.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\"" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-873bc5fc-9412-54c7-80ae-349f7825de0a"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-873b